There was a significant main effect of HMI on user experience (F(2, 46) = 10.99, p <.001) with a significantly poorer user experience of the HDD compared to HUD and AR-HUD. This effect was solely due to hedonic quality (F(2, 46) = 25.74, p <.001; Fig. 4 right), while pragmatic quality was not rated differently for the three HMI variants (F(2, 46) = 2.29, p <.113; Fig. 4 middle). Concerning hedonic quality, statistical posts-hoc testing showed that HUD was rated better than HDD and AR-HUD better than HDD and HUD (m HDD  = 1.78, 95 %-CI HDD [0.61, 1.74]; m HUD  = 1.80, 95 %-CI HUD [1.39, 2.21]; m AR-HUD  = 1.30, 95 %-CI AR-HUD [0.68, 1.92]).

There was a significant effect of display technology on drivers’ feedback concerning obstruction (F(2, 46) = 20.55, p <.001; Fig. 5 left). With AR-HUD, a considerable number of drivers (n = 7, i.e. 29 %) rather agreed with the statement that important areas of the road are obstructed due to the displayed navigation information.

