Drivers did not report a different level of distraction due to display technology (F(2, 46) = 1.49, p =.237; Fig. 5 right). 29 % of participants (n = 7) in the HDD condition, 8 % of participants in the HUD condition (n = 2), and 21 % of participants (n = 5) in the AR-HUD condition (rather) agreed with the statement that the display distracted them from the driving task.
3.2.2. Workload

The results showed a significant main effect of simulator drive on subjective workload (Greenhouse-Geisser adjustment was used to correct for violations of sphericity: F(2.94, 67.61) = 31.87, p <.001; Fig. 6 top left). The Reference drive was clearly more demanding than the Baseline drive. Post-hoc tests show that the workload during the Navigation drive (independent of the HMI condition) was higher than for the Baseline drive and lower than for the Reference drive. The HMI conditions (HDD, HUD, AR-HUD) did not differ in their level of subjective workload (m Baseline  = 21.50, 95 %-CI Baseline [15.71, 27.29]; m Reference  = 69.00, 95 %-CI Reference [59.90, 78.10]; m HDD  = 46.83, 95 %-CI HDD [38.61, 55.06]; m HUD  = 43.83, 95 %-CI HUD [35.49, 52.17]; m AR-HUD  = 44.67, 95 %-CI AR-HUD [36.92, 52.41]).

