The miss rate in the DRT also revealed a significant main effect of driving condition (Greenhouse-Geisser adjusted: F(2.07, 47.51) = 26.50, p <.001; Fig. 6 bottom left). There were significantly more misses during the Reference drive than during the Baseline drive (post-hoc test). The miss rate during the Navigation drive was between that of the Baseline drive and the Reference drive, i.e. the Navigation drive leads to significantly more misses than the Baseline drive but to less misses than the Reference drive (m Baseline  = 0.004, 95 %-CI Baseline [0.000, 0.009]; m Reference  = 0.245, 95 %-CI Reference [0.177, 0.313]; m HDD  = 0.116, 95 %-CI HDD [0.090, 0.143]; m HUD  = 0.138, 95 %-CI HUD [0.102, 0.174]; m AR-HUD  = 0.147, 95 %-CI AR-HUD [0.110, 0.184]). Again, there was no difference between the HMI conditions.

A very similar effect was seen for the reaction times in the DRT (Greenhouse-Geisser adjusted: F(1.96, 45.14) = 64.95, p <.001; Fig. 6 bottom right). Post-hoc testing revealed that the reaction times were longer for the Reference drive than for the Baseline drive with the times for the Navigation drives in between. There was no significant difference in reaction time between the HMI conditions (m Baseline  = 567, 95 %-CI Baseline [542, 592]; m Reference  = 840, 95 %-CI Reference [783, 897]; m HDD  = 728, 95 %-CI HDD [692, 764]; m HUD  = 709, 95 %-CI HUD [678, 740]; m AR-HUD  = 707, 95 %-CI AR-HUD [676, 738]).

The simulator drive had also an effect on eyes-off-road time (Greenhouse-Geisser adjusted: F(3.02, 66.34) = 87.18, p <.001; Fig. 6 top right). Post-hoc testing showed that the gaze was averted from the road significantly longer during the Reference drive compared to the Baseline drive and the Navigation drives. The Navigation drive with HDD resulted in more eyes-off-road time than navigating with HUD or AR-HUD. The Navigation drives with AR-HUD and HUD did not differ with regard to eyes-off-road time (m Baseline  = 0.18, 95 %-CI Baseline [0.14, 0.23]; m Reference  = 0.52, 95 %-CI Reference [0.48, 0.57]; m HDD  = 0.27, 95 %-CI HDD [0.23, 0.30]; m HUD  = 0.18, 95 %-CI HUD [0.13, 0.23]; m AR-HUD  = 0.18, 95 %-CI AR-HUD [0.14, 0.23]).

In summary, the four workload measures (DALI, eyes-off-road time, DRT miss rate, DRT reaction time) showed consistently that the Navigation drive was less demanding than the Reference drive. With the exception of eyes-off-road time in HUD- and AR-HUD condition, the Navigation drive was more demanding than the Baseline drive. The subjective and visual-cognitive workload measured by the DRT did not differ between the HMI conditions. Only the eyes-off-road time yielded a higher visual workload for navigating using a HDD compared to (AR-)HUD.

The next analyses focussed on the different layouts of the intersections. The results showed that navigating with AR-HUD leads to the most DRT misses compared to fewer misses in the HUD condition and fewest errors in the HDD condition (F(2,44) = 6.12, p =.005; Fig. 7 top left). The HMI × Layout interaction did not yield statistical significance (F(10,220) = 0.71, p =.716). There was no significant effect of HMI or layout of intersection with regard to the reaction time to a DRT stimulus ( Fig. 7 top right).

